# LINE ENDINGS ARE LF, EVERYWHERE, AND THIS FILE IS WHAT MAKES THAT TRUE.
#
# Lattice's house convention is LF: LF in the repo, LF in the editor, LF out of every
# export. Windows understands LF everywhere that matters (Notepad was the last holdout and
# was fixed in Windows 10 1809, October 2018), and Lattice's own artifacts are indifferent
# to it — PDF is binary, PPTX is zipped XML whose parsers normalize line endings per spec,
# HTML is browser-rendered. The one artifact where line endings are observable is the raw
# `.md` a user downloads or shares, and that should be LF like everything else.
#
# WHY A FILE RATHER THAN A CONVENTION. Before this, nothing enforced it. The tree happened
# to be clean — zero CRLF across every committed .md/.ts/.js/.mjs — but that was each
# contributor's local `core.autocrlf`, not policy. A contributor with `autocrlf=false`
# could commit CRLF and no gate would notice.
#
# WHAT IT COST TO LEARN. A CRLF deck rendered with the WRONG PALETTE through the emulator,
# silently: `lib/core/resolve-palette.js` was the one front-matter reader of ~55 whose regex
# lacked `\r?`, so a Windows-authored deck declaring `theme: cuoio` exported entirely in
# the default theme (#1349). The repair is not "add `\r?` to that one reader" — fifty-odd
# readers each independently remembering it is a design that guarantees the next one forgets.
# The repair is to normalize at every boundary source crosses, so no reader has to remember.
# This file is the GIT boundary. The AUTHORITATIVE list of the others is
# `SANCTIONED_EOL_BOUNDARIES` in `tools/check-ownership.js` — a gate, not a comment, because a
# boundary list kept in prose rots. This very paragraph is the proof: it named "two others" when
# there were six, and it took a review pass to notice.

# Normalize every text file to LF in the repo, and check it out as LF on every platform
# INCLUDING Windows. `text=auto` lets git detect text vs binary; `eol=lf` pins the working
# tree, so a Windows clone gets LF rather than CRLF and cannot commit CRLF back.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binaries git must never touch. `text=auto` detects most of these correctly, but naming
# them is cheap and a mis-detection here CORRUPTS the file rather than merely annoying
# someone. Types actually committed today (counted with `git ls-files`): 340 pdf,
# 108 woff2, 95 mp3, 36 png, 30 webp, 30 wav, 4 jpg. The rest are prospective — a format
# we don't ship yet but plausibly will, listed so the first one committed is already safe
# rather than depending on whoever adds it remembering this file.
*.pdf   binary
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.gif   binary
*.webp  binary
*.avif  binary
*.ico   binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf   binary
*.otf   binary
*.eot   binary
*.zip   binary
*.pptx  binary
*.docx  binary
*.xlsx  binary
*.lattice binary
*.wasm  binary
*.gz    binary
*.mp3   binary
*.wav   binary
*.m4a   binary
*.ogg   binary
*.opus  binary
*.mp4   binary
*.webm  binary

# A PATCH IS BYTE-SENSITIVE. `docs/patches/*.patch` is applied by `patch-package
# --error-on-fail` on install, and a hunk's line endings are part of the match. Today's one
# patch is pure LF, so `text=auto` is harmless — but a future patch against a CRLF upstream
# file would be silently renormalized on `git add` and then fail install. `-text` opts the
# whole extension out of any conversion.
*.patch -text

# CRLF TEST FIXTURES, IF ONE IS EVER COMMITTED. `* text=auto eol=lf` makes a genuinely-CRLF
# fixture impossible to commit: git stores a CR-free blob, prints a `warning:` that is easy to
# miss, and leaves `git status` clean while the working tree still has CRLF — so the fixture
# would pass locally for the right reason and in CI for the wrong one. Today every CRLF test
# builds its fixture at runtime with `.replace()`, which is better; this escape hatch exists so
# that a fixture that MUST live on disk has a correct way to.
*.crlf.* -text

# THE DECISION INDEX IS A GENERATED LIST OF UNIQUE ROWS, SO KEEPING BOTH SIDES IS THE RIGHT
# MERGE. `engineering/decisions/README.md` carries a block, spliced between two sentinels, with
# one row per note — and roughly half of all merged PRs rewrite it (44-50% across the last 100,
# 200 and 300 commits; `CHANGELOG.md` sat at the same place before per-PR fragments fixed it in
# #1593, and is now at 1%). That makes it the repo's highest-churn shared region.
#
# WHEN IT CONFLICTS IS NOT RANDOM, AND IT IS NOT RARE. Rows sort date-descending then filename,
# so a note carrying TODAY's date sorts above every existing note in its status group. Two notes
# written the same day into the same group are therefore ALWAYS immediate neighbours — one
# insertion point, one textual conflict — whatever their filenames are. Measured with real
# branches: same-date-far-apart-filenames conflicts, both-dated-today conflicts, and only a
# straddling note that already sorts BETWEEN them merges cleanly. This is the common shape, not
# a corner; #1547's mitigation closed the SILENT miscount case and left this one.
#
# `union` keeps both sides' rows. That is correct here because every row is unique to one note
# and the block's order is not asserted — `build-decisions-index.js --check` was deliberately
# weakened in #1547 to verify per-note correctness rather than byte-identity, so a union-merged
# index passes it. Verified by merging two real branches: both rows present, gate green.
#
# INSIDE THE BLOCK, A WRONG UNION FAILS LOUDLY. If two branches change the SAME note's status,
# union keeps both of that note's rows. The note file itself still conflicts (a real semantic
# conflict, correctly surfaced), and `--check` then refuses with "2 entries in the index — it
# must appear exactly once". Measured. That class of wrong merge cannot reach `main` quietly.
#
# OUTSIDE THE BLOCK IT DOES NOT, AND THAT IS THIS DRIVER'S REAL LIMIT. A merge driver applies to
# a whole file, but the gate reads only between the sentinels (`parseIndex` slices the text
# between them), and they sit at lines 130 and 695 — so the 129 lines of hand-written prose above
# the block (the Convention section, the status-lifecycle table, the ROW_CAP guidance) are under
# union with NO gate behind them. Two PRs rewording the same prose line would merge clean and
# commit both sentences back to back. Reproduced with `git merge-file --union`; exit 0, no
# conflict, both sentences present.
#
# Why that is still the right trade: the prose is nearly static while the block churns. Of the
# 150 commits in the last 300 on `main` that touch this file, 4 changed any line that is not an
# index row — and two of those four would additionally have to hit the SAME line in parallel.
# The failure is also visible rather than corrupting: a duplicated sentence in a rendered README
# reads as an obvious editing mistake in the PR diff, where a wrong CODE merge would not. If that
# ever stops being acceptable, the fix is to move the generated block into its own file so the
# driver covers only generated content — a bigger change, costed as the alternative in
# `engineering/decisions/2026-09-14-the-decision-index-merges-as-a-union.md` §3.
#
# (An earlier version of this note claimed the status case was "THE ONE case union gets wrong".
# It is the one case INSIDE the block. A review caught the prose region; the claim was an
# incomplete safety proof, which is worse than a stated limit.)
engineering/decisions/README.md merge=union
